1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,243
Your PC has a tiny battery inside it, even if it's a desktop that's always plugged in.

2
00:00:06,993 --> 00:00:13,908
It's a coin cell, usually the same kind you'd find in a watch or a car key, sitting right on the motherboard.

3
00:00:14,658 --> 00:00:22,621
Its job is to keep a small clock running when your PC is unplugged, and on many boards, to hold your BIOS settings.

4
00:00:23,371 --> 00:00:24,795
It can last for years.

5
00:00:25,075 --> 00:00:31,915
But when it dies, the PC may forget the date and time every time it loses power, or reset its settings.

6
00:00:32,665 --> 00:00:33,878
The fix is cheap.

7
00:00:34,158 --> 00:00:38,679
Replace the coin cell with the same type, with the power cable unplugged.

8
00:00:38,959 --> 00:00:41,525
More PC hardware explained on the channel.

